**Movie Premise: “Pocket Empire”**

A 17-year-old high-school senior named Jake, average build, perpetually horny, living in a normal suburban house with his distracted single dad, invents a crude handheld shrink ray in the garage using scavenged parts from old electronics, a high-powered laser diode, and a jury-rigged focusing lens. The device only works when the beam strikes the exact center of a human butt—any other body part does nothing. The target feels a sharp, stinging impact like a hard slap or bee sting right on the ass, then immediately writhes, gasps, and collapses as their entire body (and everything attached to or worn by them) compresses in under two seconds to a literal 7-inch-tall, fully proportioned, living, breathing, sentient adult human. Clothes, jewelry, phones, keys, underwear—everything scales perfectly. Only living humans and objects in direct contact shrink; furniture, floors, pets stay full-size. The ray has a hidden microphone and voice-command circuit that Jake discovers later; once activated, it lets him issue simple verbal orders that the shrunken person experiences as an overwhelming, irresistible urge to obey and feel content with their new status as a kept pet.

The accident happens on a Friday afternoon. Jake’s best friend’s mom, Elena (42, curvy, still attractive, yoga-toned), comes over to drop off a forgotten textbook. She bends to pick something up; Jake is testing the ray on a mannequin and the beam catches her square on the ass. She yelps, spins, grabs her butt, then screams as her body collapses inward. Clothes shrink with her—tight jeans, blouse, bra, panties all scale down. In seconds she is standing on the carpet, 7 inches tall, still fully adult in every proportion and biological function, voice high and thin but clear, mind completely intact. She staggers, feels the sudden vastness of the room, the towering furniture, the heat of Jake’s approaching hand. She tries to run; he cups her gently but firmly. She kicks, bites, curses. He can feel her tiny heart hammering, the warmth of her skin, the dampness of sudden fear-sweat through her miniature clothes.

Jake panics, then the hormones kick in hard. He realizes he can never give this up. He keeps her. He sets up a secure enclosure in his bedroom—an old dollhouse reinforced with clear acrylic, soft bedding, tiny toilet he fabricates from model parts, food scaled down (crumbs, drops of water, bits of fruit). Elena still needs to pee, shit, fart, menstruate, eat, sleep. The movie does not cut away: she squats in the corner of her enclosure, face burning with humiliation, as a tiny stream of urine hits the plastic tray; later she strains and produces a miniature turd that Jake has to clean up with tweezers. She farts and the high-pitched sound makes her cover her face. She still gets hungry, horny, exhausted. Jake talks to her, feeds her, handles her. At first she rages and plots escape. He discovers the mic function by accident while talking near the ray; a casual “relax, you’re safe here” makes her body go soft, her mind fill with a warm, pet-like contentment. She still knows who she is, still remembers her life, but the urge to fight fades and she starts accepting being held, carried in a pocket, set on his desk while he does homework.

A female police officer, Detective Mara Cole (35, athletic, no-nonsense), investigates Elena’s disappearance after the friend’s family reports her missing. She traces the last known location to Jake’s house. She comes by alone for a welfare check. Jake panics, aims the ray, waits for her to turn. The beam hits her firm, uniform-clad ass. She grunts, reaches back, then writhes and shrinks exactly like Elena—full police uniform, badge, utility belt, gun, all scaling down with her. She lands on the living-room rug, 7 inches of furious, armed authority figure. Her tiny service weapon is now a useless toy-scale replica. She draws it anyway, screams orders in her thin voice. Jake scoops her up. She bites his finger hard enough to draw blood; he winces, then activates the mic. “Be calm. You’re mine now.” The fight drains out of her. She still hates it, still plans, but the contentment settles in like a warm fog.

Jake cannot stop. The power, the constant visual of these fully adult women reduced to living dolls he can pocket, set on his pillow, watch perform every ordinary biological act at toy scale, feeds every teenage urge. He starts targeting deliberately. A strict teacher who failed him, a neighbor’s hot older sister who once laughed at him, a barista who never remembered his name, a gym instructor whose yoga pants he stared at for months. Each time the beam must hit the butt. Each woman feels the impact, writhes, shrinks, clothes and all, and wakes up in the vastness of his room. He builds more enclosures, then converts half his bedroom into a controlled “empire”—tiered platforms, tiny communal spaces, a central “throne” platform where he sits and surveys them. He feeds them, cleans their waste, handles their periods with cotton-swab “pads,” listens to their tiny farts and complaints. When they try to organize a revolt—climbing the acrylic walls, using scaled-down objects as tools, attempting to disable the ray—he simply picks them up one by one, issues the contentment command, and resets them. They fail. The movie shows the attempts in detail: tiny bodies straining, slipping, exhausted, then going limp with the forced acceptance.

By the third act the room holds a growing collection of shrunken adult women—mostly the ones he wanted to control and possess. They live as sentient, biological pets: they talk, argue among themselves in high voices, still need to shit and piss and bleed and sleep and feel desire. Jake walks among them like a giant, sometimes carrying several in a pocket or on his shoulder. He never shrinks men or children; the focus stays on the women he chooses. The final image is Jake lying on his bed at night, the reinforced dollhouse empire glowing softly on his desk, the women moving about inside at doll scale, some resigned, some still simmering, all content enough not to escape, while outside the world continues not knowing that a teenage boy has quietly built a private amazonian domain of living, shrunken adult women he will never willingly give up.

Tone stays grounded and physical: every shrink is a sudden, felt impact on the ass followed by visible, instantaneous compression and the immediate reality of being 7 inches tall in a full-size world. No magic sparkles, no cartoon physics—just biology scaled down, hormones amplified, and a boy who discovers absolute power over the women he wants and decides the only sane response is to keep it.
